window.AIBEMOCK={"n":5,"title":"Mock Test 5","theme":"Advanced level","kind":"Advanced paper","blurb":"A harder paper with finer distinctions, closer-reading questions and more case law. Attempt it once you are scoring comfortably above the pass mark.","minutes":210,"neg":0,"pass":{"General / OBC":45,"SC / ST / PwD":40},"asOn":"19 November 2026","q":[{"s":"Family Law","q":"Under Muslim law, a gift (hiba) of immovable property","o":["is valid even without delivery of possession, if the donee has expressly accepted the gift","is valid without a registered deed, if there are declaration, acceptance and delivery of possession","must be made by a registered instrument in every case, under Section 123 of the TPA, without exception","is valid only if it is in writing and has been attested by two witnesses to the gift"],"a":1,"e":"Section 129 of the Transfer of Property Act saves the Muslim law of gifts from the requirement of registration in Section 123. The essentials are a declaration by the donor, acceptance by the donee, and delivery of possession, actual or constructive."},{"s":"Family Law","q":"In Arunachala Gounder v Ponnusamy (2022), the Supreme Court held that the self-acquired property of a Hindu male who dies intestate, even before 1956,","o":["devolves by survivorship on the male members of the family alone, to the exclusion of the daughter","devolves by inheritance, not survivorship, so that his daughter inherits it","reverts to the Government, as there is no male heir remaining in the family","devolves on the widow alone for her life, and then on the collaterals of the deceased"],"a":1,"e":"The Court held that the self-acquired property of a Hindu male dying intestate devolves by inheritance, not survivorship, and that a daughter is entitled to inherit it and also to a share in the property that came by partition. The ruling extends to cases of succession prior to the Hindu Succession Act, 1956."},{"s":"Civil Procedure (CPC)","q":"Under the proviso to Section 16 CPC, a suit for specific performance of a contract relating to immovable property, where the relief can be obtained through the personal obedience of the defendant, may be instituted","o":["only where the property is situated, and in no other court of the country at all","only where the plaintiff resides, irrespective of the place where the property is located","only before the High Court that has original civil jurisdiction over the area concerned","where the property is situated, or where the defendant resides or carries on business"],"a":3,"e":"The proviso allows the plaintiff to sue where the property is situated, or where the defendant resides, carries on business or personally works for gain, if the relief can be fully obtained through the defendant's personal obedience. This is an exception to the general rule in Section 16."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","q":"Under Article 361(4) of the Constitution, no civil proceedings in which relief is claimed against the President or a Governor in respect of his personal acts may be instituted during his term until","o":["the expiry of his term of office, whatever notice is given","six months after a notice has been delivered to him","two months after a notice has been delivered to him","one month after a notice has been delivered to him"],"a":2,"e":"Article 361(4) requires two months' notice in writing, stating the nature of the proceedings, the cause of action, and the relief claimed. Article 361(2) bars criminal proceedings against the President or Governor during the term of office."},{"s":"Environmental Law","q":"In Indian Council for Enviro-Legal Action v Union of India (1996), the Supreme Court held that, under the polluter pays principle, absolute liability for harm to the environment","o":["arises only where the polluter has been shown to be negligent or reckless in conduct","is limited to compensation for the victims, and does not cover the cost of restoration","applies only to those industries that are owned and run by the Government itself","extends to the cost of restoring the damaged ecology, besides compensation to victims"],"a":3,"e":"In the Bichhri case, where chemical industries had polluted the village, the Court held that the polluter is liable to bear the cost of remedial measures, as well as compensation to the individuals affected, and the principle forms part of the law of the land."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","q":"In I.R. Coelho v State of Tamil Nadu (2007), a nine-judge Bench held that laws placed in the Ninth Schedule after 24 April 1973","o":["can be challenged only if they have been enacted by a State Legislature and not Parliament","are open to challenge as violating fundamental rights forming part of the basic structure","can be challenged only before the President of India, by way of a formal reference","are immune from any challenge, since the Schedule gives them total immunity in law and fact"],"a":1,"e":"The Court held that the Ninth Schedule's immunity is not absolute. Laws inserted after 24 April 1973, the date of the Kesavananda judgment, may be tested against the basic structure, including Articles 14, 19 and 21 as read with Article 32."},{"s":"Contract, Specific Relief & Sale of Goods","q":"In Bhagwandas Goverdhandas Kedia v Girdharilal Parshottamdas (1966), the Supreme Court held that a contract concluded over the telephone is made","o":["at the place where the acceptance is actually heard by the offeror over the telephone","at the place where the contract is to be performed by the parties","at the place where the acceptor speaks his acceptance into the instrument","at the place where both of the parties ordinarily reside or carry on business"],"a":0,"e":"The Court held that, for instantaneous communications like the telephone, the contract is complete only when the acceptance is received by the offeror, as in Entores v Miles Far East Corporation. The postal rule under Section 4 does not apply."},{"s":"Taxation","q":"Under Article 269A of the Constitution, the tax on inter-State supply of goods or services is","o":["levied and collected by the State Governments, and then shared with the Union in a fixed ratio by law","levied and collected by the Government of India, and apportioned on the GST Council's recommendation","levied by the GST Council, and collected by the local bodies in their own respective areas","levied and collected by the Government of India, and wholly retained by the Union itself"],"a":1,"e":"Article 269A provides that the IGST on supplies in the course of inter-State trade or commerce is levied and collected by the Government of India, and apportioned as provided by Parliament by law on the recommendations of the GST Council."},{"s":"Jurisprudence","q":"Ronald Dworkin criticised H.L.A. Hart's positivism by arguing that in hard cases judges apply","o":["only their personal moral beliefs, with no legal constraint","only commands of the sovereign, as laid down by the legislature","only rules, which are identified by the rule of recognition","principles, which are not identified by any rule of recognition"],"a":3,"e":"In 'Taking Rights Seriously', Dworkin argued that legal systems contain principles, as well as rules, which judges are bound to apply in hard cases. Principles have a dimension of weight, and are not identified by their pedigree under a rule of recognition."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS)","q":"Which of the following falls within 'cruelty' for the purposes of the offence under Section 85 of the BNS?","o":["The husband's refusal to relocate to another city for the sake of the wife's employment","A single instance of insult to the wife at a family function held at their own home","Harassment to coerce her or her relatives into meeting an unlawful demand for property","A quarrel between the husband and the wife over the ordinary household chores of the family"],"a":2,"e":"Under the Explanation to Section 86 (corresponding to Section 498A IPC), 'cruelty' means wilful conduct likely to drive the woman to suicide or cause grave injury to her life, limb or health, or harassment with a view to coercing her or her relatives to meet an unlawful demand for property or valuable security."},{"s":"Administrative Law","q":"In Ridge v Baldwin (1964), the House of Lords held that the dismissal of a Chief Constable without a hearing was","o":["valid, as natural justice applies only to those bodies that are strictly judicial in nature","invalid, as the doctrine of proportionality had been breached by the Watch Committee concerned","invalid, as natural justice applies to administrative decisions that affect a person's rights","valid, as a Chief Constable has no right to a fair hearing before his dismissal"],"a":2,"e":"The decision revived the application of natural justice to administrative actions, and rejected the narrow view that it applies only where the body has a duty to act judicially. The dismissal of an officer holding an office, without a hearing, was held to be void."},{"s":"Property Law","q":"Under Section 111(g) of the Transfer of Property Act, 1882, a lease is determined by forfeiture on breach of an express condition, if the lessor","o":["waits until the lessee has been in default of the condition for one full year","gives a notice in writing to the lessee of his intention to determine the lease","obtains an order from the High Court for the determination of the lease before re-entering","obtains the consent of the lessee's mortgagee to the determination of the lease"],"a":1,"e":"Forfeiture requires a breach of an express condition providing for re-entry, and a notice in writing by the lessor to the lessee of his intention to determine the lease. Forfeiture is not automatic on breach. The lessor must not waive the breach."},{"s":"Criminal Procedure (BNSS)","q":"Under Section 464 of the CrPC (and its BNSS counterpart), an error, omission or irregularity in framing the charge makes a finding, sentence or order invalid only if","o":["the appellate or revisional court finds that it has in fact occasioned a failure of justice","the accused had objected to the charge at the very time that it was framed by the court","the charge was framed by a Magistrate, and not by a Judge of the Court of Session","the prosecution had not been given a copy of the charge that was framed by the court"],"a":0,"e":"Section 464 is a saving provision. A defect in the charge does not vitiate the trial unless the accused was misled and there has been a failure of justice. The court considers whether the objection was raised at an earlier stage."},{"s":"Company Law","q":"Under Section 2(87) of the Companies Act, 2013, a company is a 'subsidiary' of another if the latter controls the composition of its Board of Directors or exercises or controls more than","o":["one-third of the total voting power","two-thirds of the total voting power","one-half of the total voting power","one-fourth of the total voting power"],"a":2,"e":"A holding company controls the composition of the Board, or exercises or controls more than one-half of the total voting power, either at its own instance or together with one or more of its subsidiaries."},{"s":"Professional Ethics & Advocates Act","q":"In Bar of Indian Lawyers v D.K. Gandhi PS Narasimha (2024), the Supreme Court held that","o":["advocates are liable under the Act in the same way as medical practitioners are","advocates' services are not 'service' under the Consumer Protection Act","only advocates who work for a salary are outside the scope of the Act","advocates are liable under the Act where the fee charged exceeds a notified limit"],"a":1,"e":"The Court held that a contract of personal service and the profession of an advocate, which is sui generis, fall outside the definition of 'service', so a consumer complaint for deficiency in an advocate's services is not maintainable. Remedies lie under the Advocates Act or in tort."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS)","q":"A terrorist act under Section 113 of the BNS that does not result in death is punishable with imprisonment of","o":["not less than seven years, which may extend to a term of fourteen years, and fine","not more than five years, with fine, the quantum being at the court's discretion","not less than three years, which may extend to a term of ten years, and fine","not less than five years, which may extend to imprisonment for life, and also fine"],"a":3,"e":"Section 113(2) provides death or life imprisonment, and fine, if the terrorist act results in death. In other cases, the punishment is imprisonment of not less than five years, extendable to life, and fine."},{"s":"Public International Law","q":"In the Lotus Case (France v Turkey) (1927), the Permanent Court of International Justice held that","o":["restrictions upon the independence of States cannot be presumed","a State's jurisdiction is always limited to its own territory","a State may act only where a rule of international law permits it","a State may never exercise jurisdiction over a foreign vessel on the high seas"],"a":0,"e":"The Court held that international law leaves States a wide measure of discretion, limited only by prohibitive rules. Turkey could exercise jurisdiction over a French officer after a collision on the high seas, as no rule of international law prohibited it."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS)","q":"Under Section 304(2) of the BNS, the new offence of snatching is punishable with imprisonment up to","o":["seven years, and fine","three years, and fine","one year, and fine","five years, and fine"],"a":1,"e":"Snatching is defined in Section 304(1) as sudden or quick or forcible seizure of property from a person's possession in order to commit theft. It is punishable under Section 304(2) with up to three years and fine."},{"s":"Evidence (BSA)","q":"Under Section 3 of the Evidence Act (and its BSA counterpart), a fact is said to be 'proved' when the court","o":["finds that the opposite party has expressly admitted its existence in the pleadings","believes it to exist, or finds it so probable that a prudent man would act on it","finds that at least two witnesses have deposed to its existence in their evidence","is satisfied that it has been established beyond any reasonable doubt whatsoever"],"a":1,"e":"The definition adopts the standard of the prudent man, and not an absolute standard of proof. The degree of probability required depends on the case. In criminal cases, the standard of proof beyond reasonable doubt arises from the burden on the prosecution."},{"s":"Professional Ethics & Advocates Act","q":"Under Section 36(2) of the Advocates Act, 1961, the Disciplinary Committee of the Bar Council of India may, on a reference or on its own motion,","o":["only hear appeals against the final orders of State Bar Council committees under Section 37","dismiss a complaint summarily, without hearing the advocate who is concerned in it","withdraw a proceeding pending before a State committee and then dispose of it itself without any reference","refer a pending proceeding to the High Court for a binding opinion on a point of law"],"a":2,"e":"Section 36(2) gives the BCI's Disciplinary Committee the power to withdraw for inquiry before itself any disciplinary proceeding pending before a State Bar Council's committee, and to dispose of it. This is separate from the appellate power under Section 37."},{"s":"Criminal Procedure (BNSS)","q":"The BNSS provision corresponding to Section 311A CrPC empowers a Magistrate to order any person, even one who has not been arrested, to give","o":["only voice samples, and that too only if he has been summoned as a witness","specimen signatures, handwriting, finger impressions or voice samples, for investigation","only fingerprints, and that too only if he has already been convicted by a court","only specimen signatures, and that too only if he has already been arrested by the police"],"a":1,"e":"The BNSS widens the power to include finger impressions and voice samples, and applies it to any person, including one who has not been arrested, for the purposes of an investigation or proceeding. The Magistrate must record reasons for the order."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS)","q":"Under Section 69 of the BNS, sexual intercourse by deceitful means, including a false promise of marriage, not amounting to rape, is punishable with imprisonment up to","o":["three years, and fine","ten years, and fine","seven years, and fine","five years, and fine"],"a":1,"e":"Section 69 is a new offence in the BNS with no IPC counterpart. 'Deceitful means' include inducement for employment or promotion, false promise of marriage, or concealment of identity."},{"s":"Civil Procedure (CPC)","q":"Under Section 2(12) CPC, 'mesne profits' of property","o":["exclude profits that he might have received with diligence but in fact did not receive at all","include profits that are due to the improvements which he has made to the property himself","include profits the wrongful possessor might with ordinary diligence have received, with interest","are limited to the rent that he has actually received from his own tenants, and no more"],"a":2,"e":"Mesne profits are the profits which the person in wrongful possession actually received or might with ordinary diligence have received, together with interest, but they do not include profits due to his improvements. They are damages for the wrongful possession."},{"s":"Criminal Procedure (BNSS)","q":"In Hardeep Singh v State of Punjab (2014), a Constitution Bench held that for summoning an additional accused under Section 319 CrPC, the evidence must show","o":["more than a prima facie case as at framing of charge, but short of proof beyond doubt","proof beyond reasonable doubt of the person's involvement in committing the offence","a mere suspicion that the person may have been involved in committing the offence","only the same prima facie case that is needed at the stage of framing a charge"],"a":0,"e":"The Court said that the power under Section 319 is extraordinary, to be used sparingly, and the test is more than a prima facie case as exercised at the time of framing charge, but short of the satisfaction that the evidence, if unrebutted, would lead to conviction."},{"s":"Administrative Law","q":"In Tata Cellular v Union of India (1994), the Supreme Court held that in judicial review of tenders and contracts, the court examines","o":["the decision-making process, and not the decision itself","only whether the Government has acted in accordance with the statute","the merits of the decision, as an appellate authority would","only the price quoted by the successful bidder"],"a":0,"e":"The Court held that judicial review is concerned with the manner in which the decision is made, and not the decision. The court does not sit as a court of appeal. The Government may choose the best terms, but must act fairly and avoid arbitrariness."},{"s":"Labour Law","q":"Under the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020, a 'factory' includes premises where a manufacturing process is carried on with the aid of power and at least how many workers are employed?","o":["fifty workers","ten workers","twenty workers","thirty workers"],"a":2,"e":"The Code defines a factory as premises with 20 or more workers where a process is carried on with power, or 40 or more workers where it is carried on without power. The Factories Act, 1948 had thresholds of 10 and 20."},{"s":"Interpretation of Statutes","q":"Under the doctrine of severability, where a part of a statute is declared unconstitutional,","o":["the entire statute fails in every case, whatever the nature of the invalid part may be","the statute is automatically revived as soon as it is amended by the legislature concerned","the entire statute is sent back to the legislature to be enacted again afresh in full","the valid part survives if it is separable from the invalid part and complete in itself"],"a":3,"e":"The test, laid down in R.M.D. Chamarbaugwalla v Union of India and A.K. Gopalan, is whether the legislature would have enacted the valid part without the invalid part, and whether the valid part is independent and workable. Only then is the invalid part severed."},{"s":"Public International Law","q":"In the Nottebohm Case (Liechtenstein v Guatemala) (1955), the International Court of Justice held that for a State to exercise diplomatic protection over an individual","o":["there must be a genuine link between the individual and the State of nationality","the person must have been resident there for a continuous period of at least ten years","the person must have been born in the territory of that State, and nowhere else","it is enough that the State has granted its nationality to the person by an act of State"],"a":0,"e":"The Court held that Liechtenstein could not claim protection against Guatemala for Nottebohm, who had acquired its nationality by naturalisation without a genuine connection. Nationality must reflect a genuine link of attachment, in order to be opposable to other States."},{"s":"Family Law","q":"Under Section 28 of the Special Marriage Act, 1954, a petition for divorce by mutual consent may be presented if the parties have been living separately for","o":["one year or more","three years or more","two years or more","six months or more"],"a":0,"e":"Section 28 requires the parties to have been living separately for one year or more, to have been unable to live together, and to have mutually agreed to dissolve the marriage. The petition cannot be presented within one year of the marriage under Section 29."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","q":"Navtej Singh Johar v Union of India (2018), which read down Section 377 IPC to decriminalise consensual same-sex conduct between adults, overruled","o":["Justice K.S. Puttaswamy v Union of India (2017)","Common Cause v Union of India (2018)","National Legal Services Authority v Union of India (2014)","Suresh Kumar Koushal v Naz Foundation (2013)"],"a":3,"e":"A Constitution Bench held that Section 377, to the extent that it criminalised consensual sexual conduct between adults, violated Articles 14, 15, 19 and 21. It overruled Koushal, which had reversed the Delhi High Court's decision in Naz Foundation."},{"s":"Family Law","q":"Under the Muslim Women (Protection of Rights on Marriage) Act, 2019, pronouncing instant triple talaq is punishable with imprisonment up to","o":["seven years, and fine","two years, and fine","one year, and fine","three years, and fine"],"a":3,"e":"Section 3 declares the pronouncement void and illegal, and Section 4 punishes it with imprisonment up to three years and fine. The offence is cognizable only on information given by the married woman or her blood relatives or relatives by marriage, and is compoundable with the leave of the Magistrate."},{"s":"Civil Procedure (CPC)","q":"Explanation VIII to Section 11 CPC provides that an issue heard and finally decided by a court of limited jurisdiction, competent to decide it, shall","o":["operate as res judicata later, even if that court could not try the later suit","operate as res judicata only between the same parties in the same suit itself","not operate as res judicata, because the first court had only limited jurisdiction","operate as res judicata only if the first court happened to be a High Court"],"a":0,"e":"Explanation VIII, added in 1976, says that the competence of the court to decide the earlier issue is sufficient, even if it was a court of limited jurisdiction, such as a small causes court, which would not be competent to try the later suit."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","q":"In B.P. Singhal v Union of India (2010), the Supreme Court held that the President's pleasure under Article 156(1) to remove a Governor","o":["is wholly unfettered, and no reason need ever be recorded or disclosed to the Governor","can be exercised only on a recommendation made by the Chief Minister of the State concerned","can be exercised only after an inquiry has been held by a sitting High Court Judge","is not unfettered; removal needs compelling reasons, and not merely a change of the Union government"],"a":3,"e":"The Court held that the doctrine of pleasure is not a licence to act arbitrarily. A Governor cannot be removed because of a change of government or a difference in political ideology. He is not an employee of the Union Government."},{"s":"Civil Procedure (CPC)","q":"In a mortgage suit for sale, under Order XXXIV Rule 4 CPC, the preliminary decree directs the defendant to pay the amount due on or before a day, not later than","o":["one year from the date on which the court declares the amount due","six months from the date on which the court declares the amount due","three months from the date on which the court declares the amount due","thirty days from the date on which the court declares the amount due"],"a":1,"e":"The court fixes a date, within six months of declaring the amount due, for payment. If it is not paid, the mortgagee applies for a final decree for sale. The court may extend the time for payment, in its discretion, before the final decree."},{"s":"Property Law","q":"Under Section 130 of the Transfer of Property Act, 1882, a transfer of an actionable claim is effected","o":["only by a notice to the debtor, without any written instrument being needed at all","only by a registered instrument, which must be signed by the transferor and the debtor","only by an order of a court of competent jurisdiction passed on an application","by an instrument in writing signed by the transferor or by his duly authorised agent"],"a":3,"e":"An actionable claim, such as a debt, may be transferred by an instrument in writing signed by the transferor or his authorised agent. Registration is not required. Notice to the debtor is needed to protect the transferee against payment to the transferor."},{"s":"Evidence (BSA)","q":"Which of the following is a judgment in rem under Section 41 of the Evidence Act (and its corresponding BSA provision)?","o":["A decree for money passed by a civil court in a suit on a promissory note","A decree of injunction passed between two private parties in a suit","A decree of divorce passed by a competent court in matrimonial jurisdiction","A decree for specific performance passed in a suit on an agreement"],"a":2,"e":"Section 41 makes a final judgment of a competent court in probate, matrimonial, admiralty or insolvency jurisdiction, which confers or takes away a legal character or declares a person entitled to a thing absolutely, conclusive proof as against all the world."},{"s":"Alternative Dispute Resolution","q":"Which of the following orders is appealable under Section 37(1) of the Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996?","o":["An order referring the parties to arbitration under Section 8 of the Act","An order refusing to refer the parties to arbitration under Section 8","An order appointing an arbitrator under Section 11 of the Act on an application","An order allowing the amendment of a pleading in the suit pending before the court"],"a":1,"e":"Section 37(1) lists appealable orders: refusing to refer parties to arbitration under Section 8, granting or refusing to grant a measure under Section 9, and setting aside or refusing to set aside an award under Section 34. An order referring the parties or appointing an arbitrator is not appealable."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS)","q":"Under Section 74 of the BNS, assault or use of criminal force to a woman with intent to outrage her modesty is punishable with imprisonment of","o":["not less than two years, which may extend to a term of seven years, and fine","not more than two years, or fine, or both, at the discretion of the court","not less than one year, which may extend to five years, and also liable to fine","not less than six months, which may extend to a term of three years, and fine"],"a":2,"e":"Section 74 BNS corresponds to Section 354 IPC. The punishment is imprisonment of not less than one year, which may extend to five years, and fine."},{"s":"Evidence (BSA)","q":"In Mirza Akbar v King-Emperor (1940), the Privy Council held that Section 10 of the Evidence Act, on the acts and statements of conspirators, applies to a statement made","o":["only before a Magistrate, in the form of a confession made by the maker himself","only by the leader of the conspiracy, and not by any other conspirator in the group","at any time at all, even after the conspiracy has ended or been abandoned by its members","while the conspiracy is on, in reference to the common intention, not after it has ended"],"a":3,"e":"Section 10 is based on the principle of agency. A statement of a conspirator is relevant against the others only if made in reference to the common intention, and while the conspiracy is continuing. A narrative of past events after the conspiracy is over is not covered."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS)","q":"In Virsa Singh v State of Punjab (1958), the Supreme Court held that, for clause 'thirdly' of Section 300 IPC (now Section 101(c) BNS), the prosecution must prove that","o":["the accused intended the particular injury, which was sufficient in the ordinary course to cause death","the accused intended to cause the death of the victim, and not merely the injury that was caused","the injury was caused with the use of a deadly weapon, in a public place of general resort","the accused knew that the injury was likely to cause the death of the victim in the case"],"a":0,"e":"The Court laid down four elements: a bodily injury present, the nature of the injury, that it was intended (subjectively), and that it was sufficient in the ordinary course of nature to cause death (objectively). Intention to cause death is not needed."},{"s":"Civil Procedure (CPC)","q":"Which of the following orders is NOT appealable under Order XLIII Rule 1 CPC?","o":["An order granting a temporary injunction under Order XXXIX Rules 1 and 2 of the Code","An order rejecting an application under Order IX Rule 9 for restoration of a dismissed suit","An order rejecting an application under Order IX Rule 13 to set aside an ex parte decree passed","An order allowing an application under Order IX Rule 13 and setting aside an ex parte decree"],"a":3,"e":"Order XLIII Rule 1(d) makes appealable only an order rejecting an application to set aside an ex parte decree, so that the party aggrieved by a refusal has a remedy. An order granting the application is not appealable. Orders on temporary injunctions and on restoration are appealable."},{"s":"Contract, Specific Relief & Sale of Goods","q":"Where time is not of the essence of a contract, and the promisor fails to perform by the specified time, the contract under Section 55 of the Indian Contract Act is","o":["discharged, and neither party has any further rights or obligations under it","voidable at the option of the promisee, who may then rescind the contract at once","void from the date on which the time fixed for performance had expired","not voidable, but the promisee may claim compensation for loss caused by the delay"],"a":3,"e":"Where time is of the essence, the contract becomes voidable at the promisee's option on failure to perform on time. Where it is not, the contract remains in force and the promisee may recover compensation for the loss caused by the delay."},{"s":"Civil Procedure (CPC)","q":"Under Order XXI Rule 103 CPC, an order passed on an application under Rule 97 or Rule 99 (obstruction to possession) is","o":["a mere order, and no appeal lies against it","deemed to be a decree, and is appealable as such","an order that can be challenged only by a revision","an order that is final, and cannot be challenged at all"],"a":1,"e":"Rule 103, inserted in 1976, treats the adjudication of an application relating to dispossession or obstruction as a decree, to avoid multiplicity of proceedings. An appeal lies, and the order has the force of res judicata."},{"s":"Criminal Procedure (BNSS)","q":"Under Section 107 of the BNSS, an order for attachment, forfeiture or restoration of proceeds of crime is passed by","o":["a court, on an application by a police officer with a Superintendent's approval","the Enforcement Directorate, acting on its own motion in each and every case of crime","the High Court alone, on a petition that has been filed by the State Government","the police officer himself, who may act without approaching any court at all in the matter"],"a":0,"e":"Section 107 BNSS is a new provision that enables the police to seek attachment of the proceeds of crime through the court, which gives notice to the person concerned. The application must be made with the approval of the Superintendent or Commissioner of Police."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","q":"Which of the following provisions was struck down by the Supreme Court in Minerva Mills Ltd. v Union of India (1980)?","o":["Clauses (4) and (5) of Article 368, inserted by the 42nd Amendment","Article 31B and the Ninth Schedule, in their entirety and for all purposes","Article 31A, which protects agrarian reform laws from challenge under Part III","Article 39(b), as being inconsistent with the fundamental rights in Part III"],"a":0,"e":"The Court struck down clauses (4) and (5) of Article 368, which barred judicial review of constitutional amendments, and Section 4 of the 42nd Amendment, which extended Article 31C to all Directive Principles. It held that the balance between Parts III and IV is part of the basic structure."},{"s":"Limitation Law","q":"Under Section 14 of the Limitation Act, 1963, in computing the period of limitation for a suit, the time spent in prosecuting another civil proceeding is excluded if it was prosecuted","o":["in a court of competent jurisdiction, which had decided it finally on its merits already","with due diligence and good faith in a court unable to entertain it for want of jurisdiction","in a criminal court, in which the plaintiff had appeared only as a prosecution witness","with the intention of delaying the suit, in any court that happened to be available to him"],"a":1,"e":"Section 14 requires that the earlier proceeding relate to the same matter in issue, be between the same parties, be prosecuted with due diligence and in good faith, and be in a court which could not entertain it for a defect of jurisdiction or other cause of a like nature."},{"s":"Evidence (BSA)","q":"Under Section 165 of the Evidence Act (and its corresponding BSA provision), a Judge's power to put questions to witnesses or parties","o":["is wide, but the judgment must rest only on relevant facts duly proved","may be used to lead new evidence, which the parties themselves have not chosen to lead","is available only to a Judge of a High Court or a Judge of the Supreme Court","is limited to cross-examination, which he may conduct only on the request of a party"],"a":0,"e":"A Judge may ask any question in any form, at any time, of any witness or party, to discover or obtain proof of relevant facts. The parties cannot object. The Judge must not, however, base his judgment on facts that are not relevant and not duly proved."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","q":"Which of the following amendments requires ratification by the Legislatures of not less than one-half of the States under the proviso to Article 368(2)?","o":["An amendment to Article 54, which deals with the manner of election of the President of India","An amendment to Article 39, which deals with certain Directive Principles of State Policy","An amendment to Article 21, which deals with the right to life and personal liberty","An amendment to Article 356, which deals with the imposition of President's rule in States"],"a":0,"e":"The proviso covers Articles 54, 55, 73, 162, 241 and 279A, Chapter IV of Part V, Chapter V of Part VI, Chapter I of Part XI, the Seventh Schedule's lists, the representation of States in Parliament, and Article 368 itself. Amendments to fundamental rights and Directive Principles do not need ratification."},{"s":"Professional Ethics & Advocates Act","q":"Under the proviso to Section 10 of the Contempt of Courts Act, 1971, a High Court shall not take cognizance of a contempt of a subordinate court where such contempt","o":["has been reported to the High Court by the subordinate court","has been committed by an advocate of that subordinate court","has been committed outside the courtroom of that court","is an offence punishable under the Indian Penal Code"],"a":3,"e":"Section 10 preserves the High Court's power to punish contempt of subordinate courts, but the proviso says that if the contempt is also an offence punishable under the IPC (such as under Sections 175, 178, 179, 180 or 228), the High Court should not take cognizance and the ordinary criminal process applies."},{"s":"Labour Law","q":"Under the Industrial Relations Code, 2020, the Worker Re-skilling Fund receives a contribution from the employer, for each retrenched worker, of","o":["fifty per cent of the retrenchment compensation","seven days' last drawn wages","one month's last drawn wages","fifteen days' last drawn wages"],"a":3,"e":"The employer must credit 15 days' wages last drawn by the retrenched worker to the Fund, within 45 days of retrenchment, and the amount is then paid into the worker's account. This is in addition to retrenchment compensation."},{"s":"Family Law","q":"Under Section 25 of the Hindu Succession Act, 1956, a person who commits murder is disqualified from inheriting","o":["only the property that was self-acquired by the person who has been murdered by him, and none other","only if he has been convicted and has also served the whole sentence imposed on him","the victim's property, or other property in furtherance of whose succession he committed the murder","the property of the murdered person, but only until the date of his release from prison"],"a":2,"e":"Section 25 bars a murderer, or an abettor of murder, from inheriting the property of the person murdered, or any other property in furtherance of the succession to which the murder was committed. It reflects the principle that no one may profit from his own wrong."},{"s":"Company Law","q":"Under Section 164(1)(d) of the Companies Act, 2013, a person convicted of an offence and sentenced to imprisonment for not less than six months is disqualified from being appointed a director for","o":["two years from the date of expiry of the sentence","five years from the date of expiry of the sentence","ten years from the date of expiry of the sentence","seven years from the date of expiry of the sentence"],"a":1,"e":"The disqualification applies where the person has been convicted of any offence, and sentenced to imprisonment for not less than six months, for five years from the expiry of the sentence. If he has been convicted of an offence with imprisonment of seven years or more, he is disqualified for life."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","q":"In Ashoka Kumar Thakur v Union of India (2008), the Supreme Court upheld the validity of Article 15(5) but held that","o":["the creamy layer must be excluded from OBC reservation in higher education","the reservation must not be applied to any of the Central educational institutions","reservation for OBCs must be confined to unaided private educational institutions only","the 93rd Amendment violates the basic structure of the Constitution and is invalid"],"a":0,"e":"The Court upheld the 93rd Amendment (Article 15(5)) and the Central Educational Institutions (Reservation in Admission) Act, 2006, but required the exclusion of the creamy layer among OBCs. In Pramati Educational Trust (2014), the Court held that Article 15(5) does not apply to minority institutions."},{"s":"Alternative Dispute Resolution","q":"Under Section 31(7)(b) of the Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996, unless the award provides otherwise, a sum directed to be paid carries interest from the date of the award to the date of payment at","o":["the same rate as the current rate of interest prevailing on the date of the award","two per cent higher than the current rate of interest on the date of the award","a flat rate of ten per cent per annum, whatever the prevailing rate of interest may be","five per cent higher than the current rate of interest prevailing on the date of the award"],"a":1,"e":"The 'current rate of interest' has the meaning in Section 2(b) of the Interest Act, 1978. This default rate applies unless the award provides otherwise. It is meant to deter delay in payment of the awarded sum."},{"s":"Alternative Dispute Resolution","q":"In In re: Interplay between Arbitration Agreements under the Arbitration Act and the Stamp Act (2023), a seven-judge Bench held that an unstamped arbitration agreement is","o":["not void or unenforceable; the defect is curable and is for the arbitral tribunal","void, and the court must refuse to appoint an arbitrator in every case that comes before it","valid, but only if the agreement has also been registered with the Registrar of Assurances","valid and admissible in evidence, even where no stamp duty has been paid on the agreement"],"a":0,"e":"The Court overruled N.N. Global Mercantile (2023) and held that non-stamping does not render an agreement void, but only inadmissible in evidence until duty and penalty are paid. The question is to be decided by the arbitral tribunal, and the referral court only examines the existence of the agreement."},{"s":"Intellectual Property Law","q":"The first compulsory licence under the Patents Act, 1970 was granted in India, in 2012, in respect of","o":["Glivec (imatinib mesylate), a leukaemia drug","Sovaldi (sofosbuvir), a hepatitis C drug","Nexavar (sorafenib tosylate), a cancer drug","Viread (tenofovir), an anti-retroviral drug"],"a":2,"e":"The Controller granted Natco Pharma a compulsory licence in respect of Bayer's patent on sorafenib tosylate, on the grounds that the reasonable requirements of the public were not satisfied, the drug was not available at an affordable price, and the invention was not worked in India."},{"s":"Civil Procedure (CPC)","q":"Under Section 92 CPC, a suit for the removal of a trustee of a public charitable trust may be instituted only by","o":["the trustee alone, with the consent of the settlor of the trust concerned","any one person who is interested in the trust, without the leave of the court","the District Judge, acting on his own motion in the larger public interest","the Advocate-General, or two or more interested persons, with the court's leave"],"a":3,"e":"Section 92 aims to prevent harassment of trustees by frivolous suits. The suit must be filed by the Advocate-General, or by two or more persons having an interest in the trust with the court's leave, and be in the representative character."},{"s":"Tort & Consumer Law","q":"In Sturges v Bridgman (1879), the court held that, in an action in nuisance,","o":["it is no defence that the plaintiff came to the place after the nuisance had begun","it is a complete defence that the plaintiff came to the nuisance after it had begun to exist","the nuisance can never be actionable if it has been of a long duration at the place","the plaintiff must prove that the defendant acted with actual malice towards the plaintiff"],"a":0,"e":"A confectioner's machinery had been operating for over twenty years, and a physician built a consulting room next to it. The court held that coming to the nuisance is no defence, as a prescriptive right to commit a nuisance needs twenty years of its being a nuisance to the plaintiff."},{"s":"Criminal Procedure (BNSS)","q":"Where the accused resides at a place beyond the territorial jurisdiction of the Magistrate, an inquiry or investigation under Section 202 CrPC (BNSS Section 225) before issuing process is","o":["not permitted, as the Magistrate must issue process at once","required only where the offence is punishable with death","mandatory, since the 2005 amendment, which inserted the word 'shall' in the proviso","discretionary, and a matter for the Magistrate's choice"],"a":2,"e":"In Vijay Dhanuka v Najima Mamtaj (2014), the Supreme Court held that the word 'shall' inserted in 2005 makes the inquiry or investigation mandatory where the accused resides beyond the Magistrate's jurisdiction, to prevent false complaints."},{"s":"Civil Procedure (CPC)","q":"To prevent abatement of a suit or appeal on the death of the plaintiff or appellant, an application to bring his legal representatives on record must be made within","o":["thirty days from the date of death","ninety days from the date of death","sixty days from the date of death","one hundred and twenty days from the date of death"],"a":1,"e":"Order XXII Rule 3 provides for abatement if no application is made within the period of limitation, which is 90 days from the date of death under Article 120 of the Limitation Act, 1963. The abatement may be set aside on sufficient cause under Rule 9."},{"s":"Jurisprudence","q":"The 'realist' or 'organic' theory of corporate personality, according to which a group has a real personality of its own, is associated with","o":["Hans Kelsen","Friedrich Carl von Savigny","Rudolf von Ihering","Otto von Gierke"],"a":3,"e":"Gierke argued that a group is a real entity with a will of its own. Savigny's fiction theory held that corporate personality is a legal fiction. Kelsen treated the legal person as a personification of a complex of norms."},{"s":"Evidence (BSA)","q":"Under Section 157 of the Evidence Act (and its corresponding BSA provision), a former statement of a witness may be proved to corroborate his later testimony if it was made","o":["at or about the time of the fact, or before an authority competent to investigate it","only before a Magistrate, in a statement that has been made on a solemn oath","at any time before the trial began, so long as the statement had been reduced to writing","only after the witness has been cross-examined at great length by the opposite side"],"a":0,"e":"Section 157 allows corroboration by a former statement made at or about the time when the fact took place, or before any authority legally competent to investigate the fact. A statement made much later is of little corroborative value."},{"s":"Evidence (BSA)","q":"In State of U.P. v Raj Narain (1975), the Supreme Court held that a claim of privilege under Section 123 of the Evidence Act in respect of unpublished official records","o":["is conclusive once the head of the department concerned has made the claim of it","can be made only by the President or the Governor in person, and by none else","is subject to the court's finding whether the document relates to affairs of State","applies to every document that has been marked as 'confidential' by an officer"],"a":2,"e":"The Court held that the court has the final say on whether the document relates to affairs of State and on whether its disclosure would injure public interest. The head of the department's claim is not conclusive. S.P. Gupta v Union of India (1982) developed this further."},{"s":"Property Law","q":"A mortgage by deposit of title-deeds (equitable mortgage) under Section 58(f) of the Transfer of Property Act, 1882 can be created","o":["only in villages, if the title-deeds are deposited with a nationalised bank","in Kolkata, Chennai, Mumbai and other notified towns, without registration","anywhere in India, by merely handing over the property itself to the lender","anywhere in India, but only through a registered instrument of mortgage in all cases"],"a":1,"e":"The mortgage is created by delivering documents of title to the creditor or his agent, with the intent to create a security, in the specified towns. No registered instrument is needed, but a memorandum of deposit that creates the mortgage needs registration."},{"s":"Jurisprudence","q":"The 'incidents of ownership', such as the right to possess, use, manage and the income, the right to capital and transmissibility, were set out by","o":["John Austin","Wesley Hohfeld","A.M. Honoré","John Salmond"],"a":2,"e":"Honoré's analysis of ownership lists eleven standard incidents, including the right to possess, use, manage, the income, the capital, security, transmissibility, absence of term, the prohibition of harmful use, liability to execution and residuary character."},{"s":"Criminal Procedure (BNSS)","q":"Under Section 156(1) of the CrPC, an officer in charge of a police station may investigate a cognizable offence","o":["only on an affidavit filed by the complainant","without the order of a Magistrate","only with the prior sanction of the Superintendent of Police","only on the leave of the High Court"],"a":1,"e":"For a cognizable offence, the police have the power to investigate on their own, after registering an FIR. A Magistrate's order under Section 156(3) is needed only when the police fail to act, and the complainant approaches the Magistrate."},{"s":"Civil Procedure (CPC)","q":"Under Section 148 CPC, the total enlargement of the time fixed or granted by the court for doing an act prescribed or allowed by the Code shall not exceed","o":["sixty days","ninety days","fifteen days","thirty days"],"a":3,"e":"Section 148, as amended in 1976, allows the court to enlarge a period fixed or granted by it, even after the period has expired, but the total enlargement cannot exceed 30 days. It does not apply to periods of limitation fixed by statute."},{"s":"Professional Ethics & Advocates Act","q":"Under Rule 49 of the Bar Council of India Rules, an advocate who takes up a full-time salaried employment","o":["practise only in matters that are against the interests of his employer","continue to practise before every court as he did before the employment began","inform the Bar Council and cease to practise while the employment lasts","practise only before tribunals, but not before any court of law at all"],"a":2,"e":"Rule 49 provides that an advocate shall not be a full-time salaried employee of any person, firm, government or corporation while he continues to practise. On taking such employment, he must intimate the Bar Council and cease to practise."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","q":"In Shilpa Sailesh v Varun Sreenivasan (2023), a Constitution Bench held that the Supreme Court may, in exercise of Article 142,","o":["dissolve a marriage only on the prior recommendation of the High Court that is concerned","dissolve a marriage that has irretrievably broken down, waiving the statutory waiting period","not dissolve a marriage in any case at all, as divorce is a matter for the statute alone","dissolve a marriage only if one of the statutory grounds under the HMA is clearly made out"],"a":1,"e":"The Court held that irretrievable breakdown is not a statutory ground under the Hindu Marriage Act, but it can be a basis for Article 142 relief. It set out factors, such as duration of the marriage, period of separation and pending proceedings, and said the six-month period in Section 13B(2) can be waived."},{"s":"Family Law","q":"In Rajnesh v Neha (2021), the Supreme Court directed that maintenance in matrimonial proceedings be awarded from","o":["the date on which the parties began to live separately","the date of the marriage between the parties to it","the date of filing of the application for maintenance","the date on which the court passes the order granting it"],"a":2,"e":"The Court laid down guidelines to remove inconsistency, including a mandatory affidavit of assets and liabilities by both parties, and directed that maintenance be awarded from the date of the application, and that an order on interim maintenance be treated as an order for the whole period unless modified."},{"s":"Family Law","q":"After the Marriage Laws (Amendment) Act, 1976, adultery as a ground for divorce under Section 13(1)(i) of the Hindu Marriage Act, 1955 requires","o":["direct proof from an eyewitness who saw the respondent in the very act of adultery","a single act of voluntary sexual intercourse with a person other than the spouse","proof that the other person involved was also a married person at the time of the act","proof that the respondent was continuously living in adultery with another person for some time"],"a":1,"e":"Before 1976, the ground was 'living in adultery', requiring a course of conduct. After the amendment, a single act of voluntary sexual intercourse after the marriage suffices. Proof may be by circumstantial evidence, and direct proof is rarely available."},{"s":"Land Acquisition Law","q":"Under Section 19(7) of the RFCTLARR Act, 2013, the declaration that land is required for a public purpose must be made within","o":["six months from the date of publication of the preliminary notification","twelve months from the date of publication of the preliminary notification","eighteen months from the date of publication of the preliminary notification","twenty-four months from the date of publication of the preliminary notification"],"a":1,"e":"If the declaration is not published within twelve months from the date of the preliminary notification under Section 11, the notification is deemed to have lapsed. The Government may later issue a fresh notification, subject to the Act."},{"s":"Cyber Law","q":"Under Section 65 of the Information Technology Act, 2000, tampering with computer source documents is punishable with imprisonment up to three years, or fine up to","o":["Rs. 50,000, or both","Rs. 1 lakh, or both","Rs. 2 lakh, or both","Rs. 5 lakh, or both"],"a":2,"e":"Section 65 punishes a person who knowingly or intentionally conceals, destroys or alters any computer source code, when it is required to be kept or maintained by law, with imprisonment up to three years, or fine up to Rs. 2 lakh, or both."},{"s":"Tort & Consumer Law","q":"In a civil action for defamation, the defence of justification by truth","o":["is a complete defence, though in criminal law the truth must also serve the public good","is a defence only in criminal cases, and never in any civil action for damages at all","is a defence only if the defendant is a journalist or a public servant by his office","is no defence at all in a civil action, unless the statement was also made for the public good"],"a":0,"e":"In the civil law of defamation, the truth of the statement is a complete defence, irrespective of the motive. In the criminal law, under the First Exception to Section 356 BNS (Section 499 IPC), the imputation must be true and also made for the public good."},{"s":"Evidence (BSA)","q":"The maxim 'falsus in uno, falsus in omnibus' (false in one thing, false in everything) is in India","o":["not a rule of law or even of prudence; the court must sift the grain from the chaff","applicable only in civil cases, and never in the trials of criminal cases at all","applicable only to those witnesses who have been declared hostile by the court","a binding rule of law under the Evidence Act, which every court is bound to apply"],"a":0,"e":"The Supreme Court has repeatedly held that the maxim is merely a rule of caution, and the court must determine whether the truth can be separated from the falsehood in the testimony. A conviction can rest on the part that is found to be reliable."},{"s":"Taxation","q":"Which of the following is excluded from the definition of 'goods and services tax' by Article 366(12A) of the Constitution?","o":["Tobacco and tobacco products of all kinds","Diamonds and other precious stones in trade","Alcoholic liquor for human consumption","Electricity supplied to consumers by the State"],"a":2,"e":"Article 366(12A) excludes alcoholic liquor for human consumption from GST. Petroleum crude, high speed diesel, motor spirit, natural gas and aviation turbine fuel are within the Constitution's definition, but GST on them is to be levied from a date to be notified."},{"s":"Intellectual Property Law","q":"Under the proviso to Section 9(1) of the Trade Marks Act, 1999, a mark which lacks distinctive character may still be registered if","o":["it has acquired distinctiveness through use before the date of application","it has been registered in a foreign country, in the same class of goods or services","a majority of the members of the public has said that it likes the mark in question","it has been advertised in the national press for a continuous period of at least one year"],"a":0,"e":"Section 9(1) bars registration of marks devoid of distinctive character, descriptive marks, and marks that have become customary. The proviso permits registration where the mark has, before the date of application, acquired a distinctive character through use, or is a well-known trade mark."},{"s":"Professional Ethics & Advocates Act","q":"In Bar Council of India v A.K. Balaji (2018), the Supreme Court held that foreign law firms and lawyers","o":["may appear before the High Courts in litigation, but only with the express leave of the Court concerned","may set up offices in India and freely practise Indian law before any of its courts as of right","are barred from visiting India for any professional purpose of any kind whatsoever, even briefly","cannot litigate in India, but may visit briefly to advise on foreign law and in international arbitration"],"a":3,"e":"The Court held that foreign lawyers cannot practise the profession of law in India, whether litigious or non-litigious, but on a fly-in, fly-out basis they may advise clients on foreign law and international legal issues, and take part in international commercial arbitration."},{"s":"Criminal Procedure (BNSS)","q":"Under Section 300(2) of the CrPC, a person who has been acquitted or convicted may afterwards be tried for a distinct offence for which a separate charge could have been made under Section 220(1), but only with","o":["the sanction of the Magistrate","the consent of the Sessions Judge","the consent of the State Government","the permission of the High Court"],"a":2,"e":"Section 300(2) permits a later trial for a distinct offence arising out of the same facts only with the consent of the State Government. Section 300(1) otherwise bars a second trial for the same offence or on the same facts for another offence."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS)","q":"Under Section 105 of the BNS, culpable homicide not amounting to murder, committed with the intention of causing death, is punishable with imprisonment for life, or imprisonment of not less than","o":["seven years, which may extend to fourteen years, and fine","ten years, which may extend to twenty years, and fine","five years, which may extend to ten years, and fine","three years, which may extend to seven years, and fine"],"a":2,"e":"Section 105 BNS corresponds to Section 304 IPC, but fixes a minimum of five years for the first part. For the second part, where the act is done with knowledge that it is likely to cause death but without intention, the punishment is up to ten years, or fine, or both."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","q":"In Anuradha Bhasin v Union of India (2020), the Supreme Court held that","o":["internet speech and trade are protected, and an indefinite internet shutdown is impermissible","the right to access the internet is not protected under any provision of Part III of the Constitution","an indefinite suspension of the internet is permissible if the Government so decides in its wisdom","an order that suspends the internet need not be published or made available to the public"],"a":0,"e":"The Court held that the freedom of speech and expression and the freedom to carry on trade through the medium of the internet are protected under Article 19(1)(a) and 19(1)(g), subject to Articles 19(2) and 19(6). Suspension orders must be published and be proportionate, temporary and subject to review."},{"s":"Family Law","q":"Under Section 7 of the Hindu Adoptions and Maintenance Act, 1956, a Hindu male with a living wife can adopt only with her consent, unless she","o":["is employed in a profession, and earns an independent income of her own","has no capacity to bear a child of her own, as certified by a doctor","has finally renounced the world, ceased to be a Hindu, or is of unsound mind","has been living separately from him for a continuous period of one full year"],"a":2,"e":"The proviso to Section 7 lists the exceptions: the wife has completely and finally renounced the world, has ceased to be a Hindu, or has been declared by a court of competent jurisdiction to be of unsound mind. Where there are two or more wives, the consent of all is needed."},{"s":"Evidence (BSA)","q":"To prove the execution of a will, which is a document required by law to be attested, the propounder must call","o":["the Sub-Registrar, since registration is conclusive proof of execution","both the attesting witnesses, in every case, without any exception","the family doctor of the testator, to prove his sound mind","at least one attesting witness, if alive and capable of giving evidence"],"a":3,"e":"Under Section 68 of the Evidence Act, read with Section 63 of the Indian Succession Act, at least one attesting witness must be called to prove execution. Registration does not dispense with proof of attestation. The propounder must also remove suspicious circumstances."},{"s":"Property Law","q":"Which of the following leases of immovable property may be made orally, with delivery of possession, under Section 107 of the Transfer of Property Act, 1882?","o":["A lease from month to month","A lease from year to year","A lease for a term of two years","A lease reserving a yearly rent"],"a":0,"e":"Section 107 requires a registered instrument for a lease from year to year, for any term exceeding one year, or reserving a yearly rent. All other leases may be made by a registered instrument or by oral agreement accompanied by delivery of possession."},{"s":"Professional Ethics & Advocates Act","q":"In Re: Prashant Bhushan (2020), the Supreme Court, having held an advocate guilty of criminal contempt for two tweets, imposed","o":["simple imprisonment of six months, together with a fine of Rs. 2,000 in addition","a token fine of Re. 1, failing which three months' jail and a three-year bar","suspension of his licence to practise for a continuous period of five years","a reprimand alone, and no further punishment of any other kind whatsoever"],"a":1,"e":"The Court imposed a fine of Re. 1 payable by a stated date, failing which the advocate would suffer simple imprisonment of three months and be debarred from practice for three years. The advocate paid the fine under protest."},{"s":"Contract, Specific Relief & Sale of Goods","q":"Under Section 16(c) of the Specific Relief Act, 1963, a person seeking specific performance of a contract must aver and prove that he","o":["has been in possession of the subject-matter of the contract throughout the whole period","has paid the entire consideration in advance, before he files the suit in the court concerned","will suffer a loss for which money would be an inadequate relief to him as a plaintiff","has performed, or has always been ready and willing to perform, the essential terms for him"],"a":3,"e":"Readiness and willingness is a condition precedent for specific performance. After the 2018 amendment, the plaintiff no longer has to show that damages would be an inadequate relief, as specific performance is no longer discretionary."},{"s":"Criminal Procedure (BNSS)","q":"Under the proviso to Section 389(1) of the CrPC, before releasing on bail a convict sentenced to death, life imprisonment or ten years or more pending appeal, the appellate court must","o":["obtain the written consent of the victim or of the members of the victim's family","obtain the opinion of the Advocate-General of the State on the question of release","give the Public Prosecutor an opportunity to show cause in writing against the release","wait until the appeal has been finally admitted by the High Court after a full hearing"],"a":2,"e":"The proviso, added in 2005, requires the court to give the Public Prosecutor an opportunity to show cause in writing against release. It applies to convictions for offences punishable with death, life imprisonment or imprisonment of not less than ten years."},{"s":"Interpretation of Statutes","q":"In Bhikaji Narain Dhakras v State of Madhya Pradesh (1955), the Supreme Court held that a pre-Constitution law inconsistent with a fundamental right","o":["is void ab initio, and cannot be revived by any amendment of the Constitution made later","is valid in all respects, and the fundamental right must yield to such a law","is not void ab initio but eclipsed, and revives if the constitutional bar is removed","is repealed, but may be re-enacted only by means of a fresh Act of the legislature"],"a":2,"e":"The doctrine of eclipse says that such a law is overshadowed by the fundamental right and becomes dormant, but is not dead. If the constitutional bar is removed by an amendment, the law is revived. The doctrine does not apply to post-Constitution laws, which are void ab initio."},{"s":"Professional Ethics & Advocates Act","q":"Under Section 35(2) of the Advocates Act, 1961, before passing an order on a complaint of misconduct, the Disciplinary Committee of a State Bar Council must give an opportunity of being heard to the advocate concerned and to","o":["the Advocate-General of the State","the Chief Justice of the High Court","the Registrar of the High Court","the Attorney General of India"],"a":0,"e":"Section 35(2) requires the committee to fix a date of hearing and issue notice to the advocate concerned and to the Advocate-General of the State. The Advocate-General is entitled to appear before the committee."},{"s":"Land Acquisition Law","q":"In Indore Development Authority v Manoharlal (2020), the Supreme Court held that under Section 24(2) of the RFCTLARR Act, 2013 an acquisition lapses only if","o":["possession has not been taken for five years, even if compensation has been paid by then","either possession has not been taken, or compensation has not been paid, for five years","compensation has not been paid for five years, even if possession has been taken by then","physical possession has not been taken, and compensation has not been paid, for five years"],"a":3,"e":"The Constitution Bench held that the word 'or' between 'physical possession' and 'compensation' must be read as 'nor' or 'and', so lapse requires both. It also held that the deposit of compensation in the treasury is not payment if the landowner has not been tendered it."},{"s":"Environmental Law","q":"Which of the following enactments is NOT included in Schedule I of the National Green Tribunal Act, 2010?","o":["The Water (Prevention and Control of Pollution) Act, 1974","The Public Liability Insurance Act, 1991","The Air (Prevention and Control of Pollution) Act, 1981","The Wild Life (Protection) Act, 1972"],"a":3,"e":"Schedule I covers the Water Act, the Water Cess Act, the Forest (Conservation) Act, the Air Act, the Environment (Protection) Act, the Public Liability Insurance Act and the Biological Diversity Act. The Wild Life (Protection) Act and the Indian Forest Act are not included."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","q":"In Sita Soren v Union of India (2024), a seven-judge Bench of the Supreme Court overruled which earlier decision on parliamentary privilege?","o":["Keshav Singh's case (Special Reference No. 1 of 1964)","P.V. Narasimha Rao v State (CBI/SPE) (1998)","Kihoto Hollohan v Zachillhu (1992)","Raja Ram Pal v Hon'ble Speaker, Lok Sabha (2007)"],"a":1,"e":"The Court held that bribery of a legislator is not protected by the privilege under Article 105(2) or 194(2), since the offence is complete at the time of taking or agreeing to take the bribe, irrespective of the vote or speech. The 1998 majority view in Narasimha Rao was overruled."},{"s":"Cyber Law","q":"Under Section 46 of the Information Technology Act, 2000, the Adjudicating Officer has jurisdiction to decide claims for injury or damage not exceeding","o":["Rs. 1 crore","Rs. 50 lakh","Rs. 5 crore","Rs. 10 crore"],"a":2,"e":"The Adjudicating Officer, who is an officer of the Government not below the rank of Director, decides contraventions of Chapter IX, where the claim does not exceed Rs. 5 crore. Claims above that value lie before the competent court."},{"s":"Criminal Procedure (BNSS)","q":"Under the BNSS provision on supply of documents to the accused (Section 230), copies of the police report and documents must be furnished to the accused and the victim within","o":["twenty-one days of the date of production or appearance of the accused","seven days of the date of production or appearance of the accused","thirty days of the date of production or appearance of the accused","fourteen days of the date of production or appearance of the accused"],"a":3,"e":"BNSS fixes a time-limit of 14 days for the supply of copies to the accused and the victim (if represented by an advocate), which can be done in electronic form. This corresponds to Section 207 CrPC, which had no time-limit."},{"s":"Criminal Procedure (BNSS)","q":"Under Section 219 of the CrPC, a person accused of more than one offence of the same kind within twelve months may be charged with and tried at one trial for not more than","o":["four such offences","two such offences","three such offences","five such offences"],"a":2,"e":"Section 219 allows a joint trial for up to three offences of the same kind committed within a span of twelve months. Offences are of the 'same kind' if they are punishable with the same amount of punishment under the same Section of the IPC or any special law."},{"s":"Civil Procedure (CPC)","q":"Under Section 99 CPC, a decree shall NOT be reversed or substantially varied on account of","o":["misjoinder of parties or causes of action not affecting the merits","a want of jurisdiction over the subject-matter of the suit that was tried","non-joinder of a necessary party to the suit that was before the trial court","an error that affects the merits of the case that was decided by the court"],"a":0,"e":"Section 99 protects decrees from reversal for misjoinder of parties or causes of action, non-joinder of parties other than a necessary party, and errors or irregularities not affecting the merits or jurisdiction. Non-joinder of a necessary party and defects of jurisdiction remain fatal."},{"s":"Professional Ethics & Advocates Act","q":"Which decision overruled the part of In re Vinay Chandra Mishra (1995) that allowed the Supreme Court, in a contempt case, to suspend an advocate's licence to practise?","o":["R.D. Saxena v Balram Prasad Sharma (2000)","Dr. D.C. Saxena v Hon'ble the Chief Justice of India (1996)","Ex-Capt. Harish Uppal v Union of India (2002)","Supreme Court Bar Association v Union of India (1998)"],"a":3,"e":"The Constitution Bench held that, under the Advocates Act, the power to suspend or remove an advocate from the roll rests with the disciplinary bodies of the Bar Councils. The Court's contempt jurisdiction cannot be used to take away an advocate's licence, though it can refer the matter to the Bar Council."},{"s":"Labour Law","q":"Under the Industrial Relations Code, 2020, an Industrial Tribunal consists of","o":["a single presiding officer, who is a High Court Judge","three members, one each from the employers, workers and the Government","a Judicial Member alone, in all cases without exception","a Judicial Member and an Administrative Member, who sit together as a Bench"],"a":3,"e":"The Code provides for a two-member Tribunal, with one judicial member and one administrative member, replacing the single-member Tribunals under the Industrial Disputes Act, 1947. A single member may decide certain matters, such as the settlement of specified disputes."},{"s":"Contract, Specific Relief & Sale of Goods","q":"Which of the following acts is beyond the implied authority of a partner under Section 19(2) of the Indian Partnership Act, 1932?","o":["Engaging servants for the purposes of the firm's business in the ordinary course","Submitting a dispute relating to the firm's business to arbitration","Buying goods for the purposes of the firm's business in the ordinary course of trade","Receiving payment of the debts that are due to the firm from its customers"],"a":1,"e":"Under Section 19(2), in the absence of a usage or custom, a partner's implied authority does not extend to submitting a dispute to arbitration, opening a bank account in his own name, compromising or relinquishing a claim, withdrawing a suit, admitting liability in a suit, acquiring or transferring immovable property, or entering into a partnership on behalf of the firm."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS)","q":"Under Section 112 of the BNS, petty organised crime is punishable with imprisonment of","o":["not less than one year, which may extend to seven years, and fine","not less than three years, which may extend to ten years, and fine","not less than five years, which may extend to life, and fine","not more than two years, with fine only, as the court decides"],"a":0,"e":"Section 112 covers acts such as theft, snatching, cheating, unauthorised selling of tickets, and gambling committed by a group or gang that causes a general feeling of insecurity. It is distinct from organised crime under Section 111."}]};
